WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.226
What decides whether the vault survives?

00:00:03.326 --> 00:00:07.988
Every step between seeing something and saving it is a step where we stop.

00:00:08.088 --> 00:00:11.070
And what we feed the vault sets its ceiling.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:18.314
Clip what we would want to find again in a year: primary sources, engineering posts,

00:00:18.364 --> 00:00:20.148
papers and documentation.

00:00:20.248 --> 00:00:25.654
Skip news that goes stale, listicles, and anything saved out of guilt.

00:00:26.204 --> 00:00:32.744
Video fades fast, so we pull the transcript. Keep timestamps. Clean it first.

00:00:32.794 --> 00:00:34.850
And split long talks by topic.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.310
Chat history gets sorted into three piles. Worth ingesting.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.374
Worth keeping as an archive. And delete.

00:00:43.924 --> 00:00:49.570
Four digests cover one launch. Do not treat repetition across newsletters as confirmation.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:55.138
For a big backfill, process oldest first, ten sources per batch.

00:00:55.188 --> 00:01:01.190
After each batch: update the index, append to the log, commit, report.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:07.098
That's chapter three, ingestion. Start with page one: Web articles.
